WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.046
In 1929, the name of Walter Duranty, The New York Times journalist, 
was on everyone’s lips:

00:00:06.152 --> 00:00:09.577
he was the first Western journalist
to interview Joseph Stalin!

00:00:10.764 --> 00:00:15.153
In 1930, a Cambridge graduate
and adviser to David Lloyd George,

00:00:15.255 --> 00:00:17.590
the former Prime Minister of the United Kingdom,

00:00:17.691 --> 00:00:23.003
arrived in Stalino
(the former name of Donetsk city up until 1961).

00:00:23.641 --> 00:00:25.851
He had heard his mother’s stories about this place,

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:29.734
knew Russian well,
and dreamt of visiting the USSR someday.

00:00:30.721 --> 00:00:35.520
His name was Gareth Jones, and he was unknown
to the general public at the time.

00:00:43.804 --> 00:00:49.744
In 1931, the famous reporter Duranty
posted 13 articles about the USSR.

00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:54.704
Although his views surprisingly lined up
with the official Kremlin position

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.062
and were highly admired by Stalin himself,

00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:01.621
this did not prevent him from receiving
the Pulitzer Prize and recognition.

00:01:02.147 --> 00:01:09.114
On 15 February 1932, a Ukrainian newspaper “Svoboda”, 
based in Jersey City in the U.S.,

00:01:09.465 --> 00:01:14.630
published an article under the title “Moscow wants
to starve Ukrainian villagers to death”.

00:01:15.386 --> 00:01:21.033
In 1933, more articles were published about
the famine in the USSR almost weekly.

00:01:21.295 --> 00:01:26.020
On 23 February 1933, the USSR issued a resolution

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.144
that forbade foreign journalists from leaving cities.

00:01:29.694 --> 00:01:32.449
In March of 1933, despite the ban,

00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:37.520
Gareth Jones bought a train ticket
as a private individual and went to Ukraine.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:42.812
He left the train before his final destination 
and started a long journey through Ukrainian villages.

00:01:53.151 --> 00:01:56.723
The winter of 1932 to the summer of 1933

00:01:56.978 --> 00:02:02.646
was the peak of what would be later recognised
as the genocide of the Ukrainian people.

00:02:03.235 --> 00:02:06.262
In the attempts to break the resistance
of Ukrainian villagers

00:02:06.364 --> 00:02:10.502
against collectivisation and the unrealistic
grain procurement plans,

00:02:10.612 --> 00:02:13.600
the Soviet government first took away all grains,

00:02:13.757 --> 00:02:17.979
then all food, and then limited the ability
to travel inside the country.

00:02:18.337 --> 00:02:22.202
The villages essentially turned into cemeteries
with no way to escape.

00:02:22.881 --> 00:02:26.390
Jones, once in love with the far-away industrial land,

00:02:26.572 --> 00:02:31.087
saw death, destruction, and total starvation
with his own eyes.

00:02:31.803 --> 00:02:36.465
In the same March of 1933,
a journalist Malcolm Muggeridge

00:02:36.729 --> 00:02:40.573
published three analytical articles
in The Manchester Guardian

00:02:40.714 --> 00:02:44.475
about the famine in Ukraine,
for the first time in the Western press.

00:02:44.767 --> 00:02:50.141
After that, the same newspaper published
Gareth Jones’ article “Famine Rules Russia”.

00:02:50.908 --> 00:02:54.731
At the same time,
Jones held a large press conference in Berlin,

00:02:55.001 --> 00:02:59.286
where he shared about the actual situation in the USSR with the general public.

00:02:59.821 --> 00:03:05.908
However, on 31 March 1933, The New York Times published 
something entirely different —

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:08.805
devastating criticism from Walter Duranty,

00:03:08.912 --> 00:03:12.316
the Pulitzer Prize winner
who had lived in Moscow for years.

00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:16.711
He stated that there were indeed
problems with food supply in the USSR

00:03:16.916 --> 00:03:18.979
but not as bad as Jones described.

00:03:19.642 --> 00:03:23.403
In the same way,
he justified the Soviet leaders and politics,

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.205
and called the millions of people
who died at that time

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.141
simply a running cost
on the way to progress and prosperity.

00:03:30.792 --> 00:03:32.268
This is what Stalin said of him,

00:03:41.552 --> 00:03:43.007
It could only mean one thing —

00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:48.808
Duranty played the same role in sending millions of deaths
 into oblivion as Soviet propaganda.

00:03:49.277 --> 00:03:53.520
The Western politicians
who knew the situation in the USSR fairly well,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.032
such as the President of the United States
Franklin D. Roosevelt,

00:03:57.189 --> 00:03:58.267
stayed silent.

00:03:58.796 --> 00:04:06.497
As a result, Gareth Jones became an exile,
while in 1933 the U.S. officially recognised the USSR.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:10.880
In 1935, Jones died under unknown circumstances.

00:04:11.919 --> 00:04:14.385
The civil losses in Ukraine during World War II

00:04:14.385 --> 00:04:18.720
are equal to the losses
during a few peacetime months in 1933.

00:04:18.993 --> 00:04:25.101
In 1948, the UN adopted The Convention 
on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.619
It established genocide
as acts committed with intent to destroy,

00:04:29.799 --> 00:04:34.545
in whole or in part, a national, ethnic,
racial or religious group.

00:04:35.623 --> 00:04:37.413
The first mentions of the Holodomor

00:04:37.414 --> 00:04:41.258
began appearing in Western discourse
no sooner than the 1980s.

00:05:27.155 --> 00:05:33.976
In 1983, Bohdan Krawchenko, M. Maksudov,
James Mace, and Roman Serbyn

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.595
spoke at the Scientific Conference
at the University of Quebec.

00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:43.351
At the same time, when journalists asked 
the Ukrainian diplomats in the UN about the Holodomor,

00:05:43.796 --> 00:05:45.088
they just stayed silent.

00:05:46.113 --> 00:05:52.706
In 1984, Ihor Olshaniwsky, the head of an organisation called
 “Americans for Human Rights in Ukraine”,

00:05:53.097 --> 00:05:56.331
with the support from U.S. senators Bradley and Florio,

00:05:56.584 --> 00:05:58.726
initiated a committee in the U.S. Congress

00:05:58.836 --> 00:06:02.880
that had to study the Ukrainian famine of 1932–1933.

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:07.736
Daniel Andrew Mica, a member of the U.S. Congress, 
was the head of this committee.

00:06:08.405 --> 00:06:11.599
The working group for gathering facts was led by James Mace,

00:06:11.723 --> 00:06:15.099
who worked at the Ukrainian Research Institute at Harvard University.

00:06:15.486 --> 00:06:19.044
In 1986, thanks to the materials gathered by James Mace,

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:25.938
Robert Conquest published a book called “The Harvest of Sorrow: 
Soviet Collectivisation and the Terror-Famine”,

00:06:26.488 --> 00:06:27.760
which became a bestseller.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.132
Later, Mace wrote, “My fate is such that your dead chose me.”

00:06:33.684 --> 00:06:38.355
Mace’s research was based, first and foremost,
on eyewitnesses’ memories.

00:06:38.711 --> 00:06:44.131
In April of 1988, the U.S. Congress received
the final report from the committee,

00:06:44.444 --> 00:06:47.840
where one of the paragraphs called the Holodomor genocide.

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:56.061
In December of 1987, the First Secretary of the Central Committee 
of the Communist Party of Ukraine Volodymyr Shcherbytsky

00:06:56.305 --> 00:07:00.551
finally admitted the fact of the famine in 1932–1933.

00:07:01.023 --> 00:07:06.058
However, the communists talked about natural reasons 
for the famine, particularly draught,

00:07:06.482 --> 00:07:11.578
undervalued the extent, and refuted the fact
that it was man-made and planned.

00:08:17.257 --> 00:08:22.090
The first country in the world to recognise the Holodomor as genocide was Estonia.

00:08:22.825 --> 00:08:28.366
On 20 October 1993, the Parliament of Estonia
made a respective statement,

00:08:28.696 --> 00:08:31.364
condemning the Soviet politics of genocide,

00:08:31.737 --> 00:08:37.642
and showed solidarity in remembering
the 1932–1933 Holodomor victims.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.853
As of today, 16 countries recognise the Holodomor as genocide,

00:08:43.153 --> 00:08:51.183
including Australia, Canada, Vatican City,
Poland, Lithuania, Georgia, Latvia, and others.

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:55.000
In 2003, with the assistance of James Mace,

00:08:55.388 --> 00:08:59.189
The Holodomor Victims Remembrance Day was established in Ukraine.

00:08:59.732 --> 00:09:04.160
On the last Saturday of November,
there are many candles in many windows,

00:09:04.477 --> 00:09:12.183
lit in remembrance of grandparents, parents, brothers, sisters,
and friends who died due to the genocide.

00:09:12.495 --> 00:09:21.010
On 28 November 2006, the Supreme Council of Ukraine passed a law 
“About the Holodomor of 1932–1933 in Ukraine”,

00:09:21.476 --> 00:09:26.329
according to which the Holodomor was recognized
as genocide of the Ukrainian people.

00:09:26.846 --> 00:09:31.476
For Ukrainians, it meant that the tragedy of thousands of families
finally came out.

00:09:32.015 --> 00:09:38.851
The stories about the terrible events of 1933
that were passed in absolute secret were finally told.

00:09:39.256 --> 00:09:41.256
During Viktor Yushchenko’s presidency,

00:09:41.512 --> 00:09:46.345
the Holodomor became one of the most prioritized topics
of the politics of national memory.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.921
The memorial for the Holodomor Victims
was also established at this time.

00:09:51.227 --> 00:09:56.100
Russia, as the successor to the USSR, admits the fact of the famine,

00:09:56.381 --> 00:10:01.376
but completely refuses to recognise the Holodomor
as genocide against the Ukrainian people.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:05.779
It would, obviously, destroy the notion of “brotherly nations”.

00:10:06.505 --> 00:10:10.083
The USSR’s position did not allow acknowledging millions of victims,

00:10:10.487 --> 00:10:15.664
in the same way as Russia’s position now
does not allow admitting annexation of Crimea

00:10:15.899 --> 00:10:19.255
and the undeniable occupation of parts of Donbas.

00:10:19.776 --> 00:10:25.944
Walter Duranty, who was once praising Stalin
and called thousands of deaths the necessary running cost,

00:10:26.371 --> 00:10:28.047
still has the Pulitzer Prize,

00:10:28.385 --> 00:10:33.906
although his name does not inspire ovation now
but stands next to the Soviet regime’s crimes.

00:10:34.617 --> 00:10:37.426
The books and movies about Gareth Jones are bestsellers,

00:10:37.718 --> 00:10:42.874
and he himself became a symbol of honest journalism
and truth despite everything.

00:10:43.083 --> 00:10:47.713
Is it the time for the Pulitzer Prize committee
to finally set historical justice

00:10:47.924 --> 00:10:50.814
and take the honorary award from the propagandist

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:54.254
that delayed the recognition of the genocide by many years?

